   ],
   "l": "Those of old good at being men-in-service —\nfine-drawn, subtle, dark, going through everything,\ndeep — not to be made out.\nJust because they cannot be made out,\ntherefore one forcibly makes a likeness of them:\nWary! — as if fording a river in winter,",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Those of old who were good at serving were fine-drawn, subtle, dark, and went through everything; they were too deep to be made out. Just because they cannot be made out, one can only force a likeness of them: wary, like a man fording a river in winter;",
   "n": "者 is the workhorse of Classical Chinese: it turns everything in front of it into a noun. 古之善為士者 is 'antiquity's / good-at-being-a-shi / ones'. Then 夫唯 A 故 B — 'it is only because A that B' — a formula you met at chapter 8 (夫唯不爭，故無尤), and at chapter 2 with 是以 doing 故's work (夫唯弗居，是以不去); 夫 here is read fú, the sentence-opening particle, not fū 'a man'. Textual note: the Mawangdui manuscripts read 善為道者, 'those good at practising the Way', where the received text has 善為士者. 士 is a social word — the man of the lower gentry who serves — so the two readings pull the chapter in different directions, toward a discipline or toward an office.",
   "p": true
  },
