   "l": "Hesitant! — as if in fear of the neighbours on all four sides,",
   "v": true,
   "n": "豫 and 猶 are both, at bottom, the names of animals famous for shying and doubling back; the language wore them down into words for hesitation, and later glued them together into 猶豫, still the ordinary word for 'to hesitate'. 兮 is the sighing particle of southern song — it carries no meaning at all, only breath, and English has nothing to put in its place. Note that the line before used 焉 for the same job: two particles, one function."
