   "l": "Grave! — he as if in formal bearing,\nMelting! — like ice about to thaw,\nSolid! — he as if the uncarved block,\nWide! — he as if a valley,\nMurky! — he as if muddied water.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "grave, as though standing on ceremony; melting away, like ice on the point of thaw; solid, like the uncarved block; open, like a valley; murky, like water thick with silt.",
   "n": "冰之將釋 shows 之 doing a third job. In chapter 1 it was 'of'; in chapter 9 it was 'it'; here it sits between a subject and its verb and turns the whole clause into a phrase — 'the ice's being about to thaw'. One character, three uses, nothing to tell them apart but position. 樸 is this book's great figure and its English is fixed here at the uncarved block — timber as it comes from the tree, still able to become anything, which is exactly what a finished thing cannot. First line, first doubt: our edition prints 若容, 'like formal bearing', but a great many editions and the Mawangdui manuscripts read 若客, 'like a guest' — the two characters share a roof and are easily confused in copying, and 容 has already been used four lines above. We print what the edition prints and tell you the other."
  },
