   "l": "Who is able — what is muddy — by stilling it slowly to come clear?\nWho is able — what is at rest — by long stirring it slowly to come to life?",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Who can take what is muddy and, by stilling it, let it slowly clear? Who can take what is at rest and, by stirring it long, let it slowly come to life?",
   "n": "孰 is the interrogative 'who', and it asks the question by itself — no particle needed at the end, unlike chapter 10's six questions, which all leaned on 乎. The pair is the whole point of the chapter: muddy water is not cleared by working at it, only by being left alone, and what has settled is brought back to life only by long stirring. Neither of the two clauses says what its subject is; 'the water' is the reader's to supply. Some editions run the second question differently — 孰能安以動之徐生, without 久 — and a few omit the second question altogether. The glosses read 以 as 'by means of', but some take it as 而 'and then' — 濁以靜之 as 'muddy, and then still it'. The literal above works under either; the gloss has to pick one and picks the first."
  },
