   "i": "It is just because he is never full that he can wear thin and need no making new.",
   "n": "This last line is one of the sorest places in the book, and no reading of it is safe. Our edition prints 蔽不新成 — 'worn, and not newly made', which we take as: he can go on wearing thin without ever needing to be made new. Many editions read 蔽而新成, 'worn out and yet made new again' — 不 and 而 differ by a stroke or two in old hands — which reverses the sense into renewal. Others read 敝 for 蔽. The chapter's earlier lines rhymed in Old Chinese and partly still do: 樸 pǔ, 谷 gǔ, 濁 zhuó fell in one rhyme group, and the first two of them still chime in Mandarin."
